| ≠ |−1| → not diagonal. So queen cannot reach b1 from d2 via diagonal.  \n    Can the queen move horizontally/vertically? From d2 to b1: not same file or rank. So no.  \n    So b1 is **not** attacked by the queen.  \n    Any other attacker? Black bishop on c5: c5 to b1 — Δfile = -1, Δrank = -4 → not diagonal match (|−1| ≠ |−4|). No.  \n    Black knight on e4: can it attack b1? e4 to b1: Δfile = -3, Δrank = -3 → not an L-shape (L-shape is 2+1). No.  \n    So **b1 is safe**.\n\n- K: c1-b2 — b2: is it attacked?  \n  Queen on d2 → b2: same rank! d2 and b2 are both on rank 2. Files b and d → two files apart. So yes — queen moves horizontally — so d2 → b2 is a legal queen move. Therefore, **b2 is attacked by the black queen**. Moving king to b2 would keep it in check — **illegal**.\n\nThus, **only legal move is K: c1-b1**.\n\nReason:  \nThe king is in check from the black queen on d2. Of the two king moves, only K: c1-b1 escapes the check. K: c1-b2 remains on the same rank as the queen and would still be under attack — illegal. Therefore, K: c1-b1 is the only legal and safe move.\n\nAnswer: K: c1-b1"}
